{"schema": 1, "kind": "review", "id": "rev-20261126T024337Z-72d8857e", "ts": "2026-11-26T02:50:24Z", "actor": {"worktree": "prawduct", "plugin": "3.10.0", "branch": "feature/boundary-review-per-release", "build": "e9c06c071287"}, "body": {"base_commit": "b647293b00a7566681b3814dfa9cedc260e3e57f", "base_tree": "64ddd1d4fb6997c2064140a09eede48490025fd3", "head_tree": "3f1228ab13e67cd0392b48e2b99ef8d211478e83", "head_commit": "af84ebceca216bc78afb77fbedcda90c8c2ad4f9", "dispatch_commit": "af84ebceca216bc78afb77fbedcda90c8c2ad4f9", "mode": "cumulative (bundle review, ready for merge)", "mode_chosen_by": "explicit-args", "stage": "boundary", "tier": "escalate", "roster": [{"role": "correctness", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "design", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "sustainability", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}], "files_reviewed": ["documentation/release-process.md", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/docs/governance-telemetry.md", "plugin/docs/project-state-keys.md", "plugin/lib/boundary_coverage.py", "plugin/lib/core.py", "plugin/lib/coverage.py", "plugin/lib/coverage_algebra.py", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/critic_mode.py", "plugin/lib/gates.py", "plugin/lib/pr_gate.py", "plugin/lib/release_readiness.py", "plugin/lib/review_lane.py", "plugin/lib/risk.py", "plugin/lib/telemetry.py", "plugin/methodology/planning.md", "plugin/skills/critic/SKILL.md", "plugin/skills/critic/review-cycle.md", "plugin/skills/critic/review-protocol.md", "plugin/skills/pr/SKILL.md", "plugin/templates/project-state.yaml", "tests/test_batch_review_rule.py", "tests/test_boundary_coverage.py", "tests/test_fix_tail_deferral.py", "tests/test_per_release_lane_inference.py", "tests/test_release_batch_review.py", "tests/test_release_gate.py", "tests/test_release_readiness.py", "tests/test_review_lane.py", "tests/test_review_stats.py", "tests/test_reviewer_payload_budget.py", "tests/test_v5_methodology.py"], "files_changed": [".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/architecture.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/boundary-patterns.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-boundary-review-per-release.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/nonfunctional-requirements.md", ".prawduct/changes/boundary-coverage-transfer.md", ".prawduct/changes/boundary-review-per-release.md", ".prawduct/owner-rulings.md", ".prawduct/runbooks/cut-and-publish-a-plugin-release.md", "documentation/release-process.md", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/docs/governance-telemetry.md", "plugin/docs/project-state-keys.md", "plugin/lib/boundary_coverage.py", "plugin/lib/core.py", "plugin/lib/coverage.py", "plugin/lib/coverage_algebra.py", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/critic_mode.py", "plugin/lib/gates.py", "plugin/lib/pr_gate.py", "plugin/lib/release_readiness.py", "plugin/lib/review_lane.py", "plugin/lib/risk.py", "plugin/lib/telemetry.py", "plugin/methodology/planning.md", "plugin/skills/critic/SKILL.md", "plugin/skills/critic/review-cycle.md", "plugin/skills/critic/review-protocol.md", "plugin/skills/pr/SKILL.md", "plugin/templates/project-state.yaml", "tests/test_batch_review_rule.py", "tests/test_boundary_coverage.py", "tests/test_fix_tail_deferral.py", "tests/test_per_release_lane_inference.py", "tests/test_release_batch_review.py", "tests/test_release_gate.py", "tests/test_release_readiness.py", "tests/test_review_lane.py", "tests/test_review_stats.py", "tests/test_reviewer_payload_budget.py", "tests/test_v5_methodology.py"], "files_oracle": [".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/architecture.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/boundary-patterns.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-boundary-review-per-release.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/nonfunctional-requirements.md", ".prawduct/changes/boundary-coverage-transfer.md", ".prawduct/changes/boundary-review-per-release.md", ".prawduct/owner-rulings.md", ".prawduct/runbooks/cut-and-publish-a-plugin-release.md"], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "The release gate's remedy cannot terminate for a two-parent `unknown` merge: the batch review it prescribes is never asked of one", "recommendation": "Class: the gate's remedy and the classifier's credit disagree about which entries a batch review can credit. `_Classifier.classify_known` returns any `unknown` with at most two parents before asking `_batch`, so batch credit is reserved for octopus merges. But `release_subject` treats such an entry as creditable, because it has a commit whose first parent is on the line, and dispatches a batch review that rates its files. `release_gate` then tells the operator to run that review. Afterwards the entry still reads `unknown`, and the per_release release stays held. Running the remedy again changes nothing, and the only way out is to drop `per_release`. The permanent member is a merge whose side shares no merge-base with the base (`--allow-unrelated-histories`, a subtree import: 'the merged branch shares no merge-base with the base'). The transient members are 'git could not diff the merged branch' and 'git could not read the merged branch's trees'. The false-positive audit table in project-state-keys.md lists none of them. Fix it through one owner: either let `classify_known` ask `_batch` of every `unknown` that names a commit with a first parent on the line (what the merge brought onto the base is what the review rates, the reasoning the octopus case already uses), or have `release_subject` and `release_gate` mark these entries uncreditable with a route that works. Add the class to the audit table, and add a test that runs the gate, then the batch review, then the gate again on an unrelated-history merge.", "files": ["plugin/lib/boundary_coverage.py", "plugin/docs/project-state-keys.md"], "scope": "class", "closure": "a release entry `release_subject` counts as creditable, which `classify_known` returns before the batch rule is asked", "members": [{"id": "m1", "site": "plugin/lib/boundary_coverage.py _Classifier._classify_shape: 'the merged branch shares no merge-base with the base'"}, {"id": "m2", "site": "plugin/lib/boundary_coverage.py _Classifier._classify_shape: 'git could not diff the merged branch' / 'git could not read the merged branch's trees'"}], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "warning", "title": "A merge whose branch carries an unresolved BLOCKING finding is credited `batch-covered` without that finding ever being resolved", "recommendation": "Instance, in the batch rule's wiring. `_classify_shape` reads a merge `uncovered` when 'an unresolved blocking finding stands on the branch's coverage'. `classify_known` then asks `_batch`, and `coverage_algebra.batch_review_covers` checks blockers only at the batch review's own two trees (the anchor and the base's HEAD). So a release batch review that rates the merge's files, with no blocker of its own, credits the merge `batch-covered`. The branch's BLOCKING finding stays unresolved in the store, and the release gate exits 0. The reviewer is never told about it either: the dispatch notes list the owed entries' labels, not their reasons. The NFR amendment this plan ratified says 'BLOCKING findings clear only through `verify-resolutions`, at either point'. Here one clears by being outlived. The PR gate refuses a blocked branch, so this needs a merge that bypassed it, and catching bypassed merges is what the release check is for. Fix: in `classify_known`, do not batch-credit an entry whose shape reading was blocked until that blocker has a resolution, or have `batch_review_covers` also settle blockers at the merged branch's trees. Add a fixture test: a branch merged with a BLOCKING finding, then a clean batch review over it, must not read covered.", "files": ["plugin/lib/boundary_coverage.py", "plugin/lib/coverage_algebra.py"], "scope": "instance", "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "The release gate's fail-closed fallback when the policy read crashes has no test", "recommendation": "Instance. `release_readiness._boundary_coverage_check` catches any exception from `review_lane.release_policy` and rebuilds the policy from this checkout's `boundary_review:`, so a per_release repo stays gated. That is the release gate's only fail-closed path for a crashed policy read, and nothing exercises it. The lane's and the fix tail's crash paths are pinned (the `boom` monkeypatches in test_per_release_lane_inference.py and test_fix_tail_deferral.py), but the release side is not. A regression that made this branch read `per_pr`, or raise, would turn a blocking release check into a pass or a traceback with every test green. Add a test that monkeypatches `release_policy` to raise in a fixture declaring `per_release` with an unreviewed merge, and asserts `check_releasability` exits 1 with the `boundary-uncovered:` line and the 'could not be read at the integration branch' note.", "files": ["plugin/lib/release_readiness.py"], "scope": "instance", "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "Whether the owed boundary review is the lane's is computed by review_owed_after, then thrown away and re-derived by each consumer", "recommendation": "Class. Why it broke: review_owed_after works out whether the per-release lane holds, but its return value only says OWED_BOUNDARY (in its second arm the lane shows up only inside the `why` prose; in its first arm it returns None). So each consumer that needs to know which review is owed calls lane_eligibility again and picks its own wording. extension_deferral_owed re-asks lane_eligibility after review_owed_after returns. critic_consolidate._plan_owes_a_later_review re-asks it to make up OWED_LANE, a value whose own docstring says it is 'Not an answer review_owed_after gives'. ShortPlanDeferral.lane is None until each caller remembers with_lane, and an un-asked deferral silently falls back to the per-PR 'boundary `cumulative`' wording. Chunk 06's change record has to fix one consumer of this kind ('The review close names the lane's review where the lane holds, not a boundary `cumulative`'), which shows the class has already caused a bug once. Every re-ask also re-runs the git work: the policy read at the merge-base and the tip, the path listing, and risk surfaces read at two revisions. A close or Stop can pay for it two or three times. Fix: have the owner return the answer (the hook-surface rule 'Export the ANSWER, not the walker'). review_owed_after should return OWED_LANE itself, and short_plan_deferral should fill `lane` itself instead of relying on a separate with_lane step. Consumers then branch on the returned kind and never call lane_eligibility. To find members, grep for lane_eligibility( and with_lane( outside critic_mode's own predicate.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_mode.py", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook"], "scope": "class", "closure": "A site outside lane_eligibility's owner that re-asks whether the lane holds to choose between the lane's review and the boundary cumulative after a function that already decided it.", "members": [{"id": "m1", "site": "plugin/lib/critic_mode.py review_owed_after (returns OWED_BOUNDARY with the lane only in prose)"}, {"id": "m2", "site": "plugin/lib/critic_mode.py extension_deferral_owed (re-asks lane_eligibility)"}, {"id": "m3", "site": "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py _plan_owes_a_later_review (re-asks lane_eligibility to synthesize OWED_LANE)"}, {"id": "m4", "site": "plugin/lib/critic_mode.py ShortPlanDeferral.lane / with_lane (tri-state filled by each caller: _mid_plan_answer, infer_mode, review_owed_after, prawduct-hook _short_plan_deferral)"}], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "boundary_review policy readings are combined, and their failure fallback re-implemented, outside review_lane, which is meant to be the key's one reader", "recommendation": "Class. Why it broke: review_lane only provides per-revision readers. So each caller that needs a combined reading builds it itself, and each one does it differently. gates._fix_tail_deferral builds its own rule for a merged branch: policy_at(merge_base) AND policy_at(branch tip). release_readiness._boundary_coverage_check wraps release_policy in a broad except. That except re-reads BOUNDARY_REVIEW_KEY from the checkout and constructs review_lane.Policy itself, which duplicates the 'checkout's per_release stands' fallback that release_policy already has. `check-boundary-coverage --gate` calls release_policy with no wrapper. So the two readers that release_gate's docstring calls 'the one answer' behave differently when the policy read fails: one falls back, the other raises. core.py's comment on BOUNDARY_REVIEW_KEY ('review_lane.boundary_review_policy is its one reader') is false at both sites (the hook-surface rule 'A docstring or comment is a claim: before one states reach or a guarantee ('the one reader', ...) grep the callers'). Fix by building it through one owner. Give review_lane a merged-branch reader, such as boundary_review_policy_of_merge(merge_base, tip). Make release_policy never raise, with its fallback inside it. Then delete release_readiness's except-branch copy, so the release check and `--gate` cannot disagree. To find members, grep BOUNDARY_REVIEW_KEY, Policy( and boundary_review_policy_at( outside review_lane.py.", "files": ["plugin/lib/gates.py", "plugin/lib/release_readiness.py", "plugin/lib/review_lane.py", "plugin/lib/core.py", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook"], "scope": "class", "closure": "A site outside review_lane that combines boundary_review readings across revisions, reads BOUNDARY_REVIEW_KEY itself, or constructs a Policy as a fallback.", "members": [{"id": "m1", "site": "plugin/lib/gates.py _fix_tail_deferral merged-branch policy conjunction"}, {"id": "m2", "site": "plugin/lib/release_readiness.py _boundary_coverage_check except-branch fallback"}, {"id": "m3", "site": "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook cmd_check_boundary_coverage --gate (no fallback, so it diverges from m2)"}, {"id": "m4", "site": "plugin/lib/core.py BOUNDARY_REVIEW_KEY comment claiming one reader"}], "fid": "R-5"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "risk._surfaces_at adds a third git runner with no timeout, on the PR-gate and Stop-gate path", "recommendation": "Instance. Why it broke: _surfaces_at calls subprocess.run itself for `git show` and `git rev-parse`. It does not use risk._git_lines, which is in the same module and has timeout=30 plus exception handling, or evidence.run_git, whose docstring says callers borrow the runner rather than growing their own. af84ebce's fix for decoding is a symptom: it patched one property of the private runner, and the runner still has no timeout. _surfaces_at runs inside fix_tail_deferral, which check-cumulative-critic and the Stop gate call, and inside lane_eligibility, which runs on every inference. A git call that hangs (a held lock, a slow filesystem) therefore hangs those gates with no limit. Fix: route both calls through evidence.run_git, or through _git_lines with a non-stripping variant for file content, and delete the local `show` closure.", "files": ["plugin/lib/risk.py"], "scope": "instance", "members": [{"id": "m1", "site": "plugin/lib/risk.py _surfaces_at"}], "fid": "R-6"}, {"goal": "Framework Check 8: Instruction Clarity", "severity": "warning", "title": "Critic SKILL lists `--span release` but never says when to pass it; the remedies send the operator to `/prawduct:critic cumulative`", "recommendation": "Instance. Why it broke: the release batch review is only reached by an explicit `--span release` flag. `boundary-uncovered:` (release_gate), project-state-keys.md and pr/SKILL.md all tell the operator to run `/prawduct:critic cumulative`, 'dispatched as critic-begin --mode cumulative --span release'. But critic/SKILL.md step 1 forwards arguments to infer-critic-mode, which returns no span. Step 4 then lists `[--span release]` with the single line '`--span release` is the release batch `cumulative`', and gives no condition for adding it. A fork that follows the skill literally on the integration branch's tip runs a plain cumulative over an empty merge-base\u2192HEAD interval. That is exit 3, which the skill says is success ('stop: do not re-dispatch'), while the release check still refuses. Fix: give step 4 a mechanical trigger, for example 'pass `--span release` when the invocation arguments contain `release`, or when the checkout is the integration base and the mode is `cumulative`'. Better still, have infer-critic-mode or critic-begin derive it, so no model chooses it. Make the remedies name the exact invocation that reaches it.", "files": ["plugin/skills/critic/SKILL.md", "plugin/lib/boundary_coverage.py", "plugin/skills/pr/SKILL.md"], "scope": "instance", "members": [{"id": "m1", "site": "plugin/skills/critic/SKILL.md step 4 `--span release`"}], "fid": "R-7"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "warning", "title": "The lane's and the release op's reasons for not relaxing are thrown away, so a per_release repo pays the moved round without being told why", "recommendation": "Scope: class. Why it broke: the new relaxation predicates return a reason, or fill `why`, for both 'not eligible' and 'could not be evaluated', but two of their callers drop it. The rule that applies is hook-surface's 'Pick each fallback's direction from the invariant it protects: an unknown bucket blocks, degraded advice names its consequence, and a swallow is fixed in the frame that discards it'. The hook's own `_short_plan_deferral` says the same thing in a comment: 'Fail closed AND say so: a crashed predicate otherwise reads exactly like an ineligible plan'. (m1) `critic_mode._lane_interval` returns None when `lane_eligibility(...)` is not eligible. `_lane_answer` and `_explicit_mode` then fall through to `rule-2 cumulative: \u2026` / `explicit-args \u2026 redirected`, and nothing says why the lane did not hold. Say a `per_release` repo hits an 11th judgeable file, a held risk surface, or a crash inside `_lane_eligibility` (which the broad except turns into a reason string). The builder is shown the boundary `cumulative` the owner opted out of and cannot tell why. A crash looks exactly like an ineligible plan. (m2) `boundary_coverage._Classifier._classify_shape` calls `gates.fix_tail_deferral` without `why`. A crash in the deferral at release time therefore reads `uncovered` with the reason 'no review evidence composes over the branch (if it was reviewed in another clone, `prawduct-hook evidence sync` brings the reviews here)', which sends the operator to the wrong remedy. Meanwhile the PR gate passes `why` and prints `deferral_unavailable`. Fix it in the frame that discards the reason. When the policy in force is `per_release`, add `lane.reason` to the cumulative rationale wherever the lane was asked and did not hold. Pass a `why` list from `_classify_shape` and add its sentence to the entry's reason. Pin each with a test where the predicate raises under `per_release` and the reason appears in the rendered output. Under `per_pr` the rationale stays unchanged, so the default adds no noise.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_mode.py", "plugin/lib/boundary_coverage.py"], "scope": "class", "closure": "A caller of `critic_mode.lane_eligibility` or `gates.fix_tail_deferral` whose answer decides which review is owed or how a release entry is classified, and which discards the predicate's reason or `why` sentence.", "members": [{"id": "m1", "site": "plugin/lib/critic_mode.py _lane_interval / _lane_answer / _explicit_mode (lane not eligible -> cumulative with no lane reason)"}, {"id": "m2", "site": "plugin/lib/boundary_coverage.py _Classifier._classify_shape (fix_tail_deferral called without why)"}], "fid": "R-8"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "warning", "title": "Chunk 03-06 design calls made for the owner are missing from the plan's inferred-call list, including a change to the owner-ruled round-budget cadence", "recommendation": "Scope: class. Why it broke: the plan's Goals list the calls 'made on the owner's behalf; the boundary review should check each', and its Governance Checkpoints point the cumulative at 'each inferred call'. But 'Decisions made mid-build: (appended while building)' is still empty, and only Chunk 03's release-span-blocker call was appended (f7042ebb). The later calls have their why only in docstrings and the change fragment, which the owner does not read to veto. (m1) `critic_consolidate._cadence_round_ids` exempts every `span: release` review from `review_round_budget`. That widens the not-counted set the owner ruled on 2026-11-23, item 2 in owner-rulings.md. The key's own entry in `plugin/docs/project-state-keys.md` (`review_round_budget`) still says 'a second `cumulative`, and a review naming no chunk are counted', and a release batch review is both. (m2) `review_lane.release_policy` lets the checkout's `per_release` stand when the tip cannot be read. The plan's architecture disposition says 'an unreadable `boundary_review:` reads per_pr'. (m3) The release span starts before the anchor, at the oldest owed entry's first parent, where the inferred call says 'release anchor tree \u2192 HEAD tree'. (m4) The batch rule credits octopus merges. (m5) A squash merge whose PR review spans exactly what it landed reads `batch-covered`. The plan's accepted tradeoff still says 'a squash-merging repo reads every merge `direct`, so under per_release it would need a batch review every release'. Each of these calls may well be right. The fix is to append each one, with its why, to the plan's mid-build decisions as an inferred call the owner can veto. m1 changes an owner-ruled rule, so it needs the owner's own word, and its key documentation must say a release batch review is not charged.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/review_lane.py", "plugin/lib/boundary_coverage.py", "plugin/docs/project-state-keys.md"], "scope": "class", "closure": "A behaviour choice built in Chunks 03-06 that the plan's Goals neither state nor list as an inferred call, so the owner's veto surface does not show it.", "members": [{"id": "m1", "site": "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py _cadence_round_ids (release batch review never charged) + plugin/docs/project-state-keys.md review_round_budget"}, {"id": "m2", "site": "plugin/lib/review_lane.py release_policy (unreadable tip -> checkout's per_release stands)"}, {"id": "m3", "site": "plugin/lib/boundary_coverage.py _release_subject (span start before the anchor)"}, {"id": "m4", "site": "plugin/lib/boundary_coverage.py _Classifier.classify_known (octopus merge asked of the batch rule)"}, {"id": "m5", "site": "plugin/lib/boundary_coverage.py batch rule over single-parent commits (squash -> batch-covered)"}], "fid": "R-9"}], "counts": {"blocking": 0, "warning": 9, "note": 0}, "observations": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "fix_tail_deferral's docstring under-states the live-branch policy read", "recommendation": "Condition 1 says the live-branch read is `boundary_review_policy` '(the merge-base and the working tree)'. That reader also requires the integration branch's tip. The code is right and nothing reads the sentence. No edit is wanted beyond making it relational ('as `boundary_review_policy` reads it') if the docstring is touched anyway.", "files": ["plugin/lib/gates.py"], "oid": "O-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "title": "A second reader of `boundary_review:` beside the 'one reader'", "recommendation": "core.py's constant comment and the plan's 'every fact has one home' disposition name `review_lane.boundary_review_policy` as the key's one reader. The release side reads it through `release_policy` and `boundary_review_policy_at`, and the crash fallback in `release_readiness._boundary_coverage_check` reads the key directly with `read_scalar_yaml_key`. All of them parse through `read_scalar_yaml_text`, so they cannot diverge on parsing, and the disposition holds in substance. The 'one reader' phrasing is inexact.", "files": ["plugin/lib/core.py", "plugin/lib/release_readiness.py"], "oid": "O-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "A squash commit that adds a release file anchors on itself and is never walked", "recommendation": "Candidate A takes the newest first-parent commit that adds a release file, and the walk starts strictly after it. A single-parent commit that adds `.prawduct/releases/<v>.md` along with judgeable code (a squash-merged release branch) therefore hides its own code from the walk. Only a pending fragment would still catch it. The plan already accepts that squash-merging repos are poorly served. Worth a row in the audit table if a squashing consumer opts in.", "files": ["plugin/lib/boundary_coverage.py"], "oid": "O-3"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "title": "Governing-artifact reconciliation: the recorded dispositions hold against the diff", "recommendation": "Checked against the code: the lane, the deferral and the batch credit are opt-in and read from the integration branch (merge-base plus tip for a PR, the tip for the release, and the checkout only where that is stricter). Every degradation lands not covered, and `release_gate` refuses it. The release batch fact is excluded in `review_edges`, so it never becomes an edge. `check-boundary-coverage` keeps exits 0/3, and `--gate` adds 1. JSON and class changes are additive. The op stays read-only apart from the existing memo. The two dispositions with soft spots are 'BLOCKING findings clear only through verify-resolutions' (the second warning) and 'one reader' (the observation above). No other conforms/inapplicable disposition is contradicted.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-boundary-review-per-release.md"], "oid": "O-4"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "Test fixture helpers copied verbatim between two new or changed test files", "recommendation": "tests/test_release_batch_review.py re-declares _git, _write, _commit, _tree, _fragment, _review, _branch, _merge and _cut, near-identical to tests/test_boundary_coverage.py's. tests/test_release_gate.py and tests/test_fix_tail_deferral.py import their helpers from sibling test modules instead. When a fixture's shape changes (for example the fragment schema), it now has to be changed in two copies. Consider importing from one module, as the other new files do.", "files": ["tests/test_release_batch_review.py", "tests/test_boundary_coverage.py"], "oid": "O-5"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "More cross-module use of private helpers", "recommendation": "New code reaches into other modules' private helpers: coverage._state_in and coverage._resolve_base_branch (review_lane, gates), risk._review_scope_paths (critic_mode), release_verification._read_declaration and gates._cached_diff_fn/_tree_key_fn (boundary_coverage). The pattern already exists in this repo. Each new reach couples another module to an internal; promote the ones that now have several external callers (_state_in has four).", "files": ["plugin/lib/review_lane.py", "plugin/lib/critic_mode.py", "plugin/lib/boundary_coverage.py"], "oid": "O-6"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "Three local copies of the same git closure inside boundary_coverage", "recommendation": "_Classifier._git, _resolve_anchor's nested git(), and _release_subject's nested git() are the same run_git-strip-or-None wrapper. _release_subject also rebuilds the first-parent position index that _Classifier._line_index already computes. A module-level helper would remove the copies. Minor.", "files": ["plugin/lib/boundary_coverage.py"], "oid": "O-7"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "project-state-keys.md: 'the branch's tip' is ambiguous", "recommendation": "'a PR needs it at both the merge-base and the branch's tip' means the integration branch's tip, as review_lane.boundary_review_policy reads it (plus the working tree). A reader could take it as the feature branch's tip. Wording only; nothing reads it.", "files": ["plugin/docs/project-state-keys.md"], "oid": "O-8"}, {"goal": "Framework Check 7: Generality", "title": "Release anchor recognizes only version-shaped tags and release files", "recommendation": "A per_release repo that tags releases by date or by name gets no anchor, so `boundary-unevaluable` on every release. The remedy covers this (tag vX.Y.Z or cut with cut-change-log), and the key is opt-in, so this is only slight specificity.", "files": ["plugin/lib/boundary_coverage.py"], "oid": "O-9"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "Branch-coverage statuses stay bare string literals across modules", "recommendation": "`deferred` joins covered/transferred/blocked/uncovered as literals compared in gates, critic_mode, critic_consolidate and pr_gate. Each reader is safe by default (an unknown status is not covered), but a typo in a reader would go unnoticed. Named constants would let a grep enumerate the readers. This follows the existing pattern.", "files": ["plugin/lib/gates.py", "plugin/lib/critic_mode.py", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/pr_gate.py"], "oid": "O-10"}, {"goal": "Framework Check 9: Cumulative Health", "title": "critic SKILL.md step 4 keeps growing as one paragraph", "recommendation": "Step 4 now covers dispatch flags, --span, --chunks including the lane exception, --scope, sweep refusal and the exit table lead-in in one paragraph. That is not wrong yet, but the conditional instructions (such as the lane's --chunks exception) are getting buried in it.", "files": ["plugin/skills/critic/SKILL.md"], "oid": "O-11"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "The PR gate's fix-tail deferral writes no record, unlike the transfer grant it sits beside", "recommendation": "`_branch_coverage` records a base-advance transfer grant because 'the transfer's yield has to be countable', and gates.py says 'Authority records its own yield'. The `deferred` pass is the same kind of authority relaxation and records nothing. At release a merge reads `tail-deferred` only until a batch review credits it, and then it reads `batch-covered`. After that the ledger cannot answer how many tails the PR gate deferred, or whether a deferred tail later drew a blocker. That is the false-negative question for #1222's relaxation. This is worth weighing before a consumer repo re-runs the pay test.", "files": ["plugin/lib/gates.py"], "oid": "O-12"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "Refusals of `--span release` are not recorded as guard refusals", "recommendation": "`begin_review` records the nothing-owed exit 3 (`critic-dispatch-release-nothing-owed`). The exit-1 refusals (`off-base`, `dirty-tree`, `no-anchor`, `unevaluable`, `no-subject`) return before any record is written. `no-subject` can hold a `per_release` release whose only way out is switching the integration branch to `per_pr`, so how often it fires is not countable.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py"], "oid": "O-13"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "`check-boundary-coverage --gate` calls `review_lane.release_policy` unguarded, while check-releasability wraps the same call", "recommendation": "If `release_policy` raises, `--gate` prints a traceback and exits 1, which the PR skill's release guard reads as `boundary-uncovered:`. The result is still closed, but the cause is misattributed. `_boundary_coverage_check` already has the attributed fallback, so the two callers could share it.", "files": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/lib/release_readiness.py"], "oid": "O-14"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "Records Pass: all 9 oracle files rated, none clears a bar", "recommendation": "Rated against the three bars (it ships / misleads into action / must stop the merge): .prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md, architecture.md, boundary-patterns.md, build-plan-boundary-review-per-release.md, nonfunctional-requirements.md, .prawduct/changes/boundary-coverage-transfer.md, .prawduct/changes/boundary-review-per-release.md, .prawduct/owner-rulings.md, .prawduct/runbooks/cut-and-publish-a-plugin-release.md. I checked each against the code: the api-contract exits and refusal codes, the consumer note's strings, the NFR bound of 10, the runbook's fourth outcome, and the boundary-patterns sweep, which returns nothing. They agree. Imprecise but inert: architecture.md and boundary-patterns.md say the release op reads 'the PR gate's recorded grant', but `recorded_transfer_grant` also accepts the Stop gate's (`_GRANT_GATES`). Nobody acts wrongly on that, and no edit is wanted. The plan's stale tradeoff and disposition are folded into the Goal 5 finding, which is about the code calls they describe.", "oid": "O-15"}], "duration_seconds": 900, "scope": "boundary-review-per-release", "scope_chosen_by": "binding", "scope_unresolved_cause": null, "scope_reason": null, "base_extended_from": null, "chunks": ["01", "02", "03", "04", "05", "06"], "chunks_proposed": ["03", "04", "05", "06"], "chunks_declared": true, "chunks_evidence": {"03": {"paths": [], "paths_more": 0, "commits": ["1cf907ca44f8", "38356ba7bec1"]}, "04": {"paths": [], "paths_more": 0, "commits": ["b57936504449", "4863d462a2d5"]}, "05": {"paths": [], "paths_more": 0, "commits": ["d45cf35d1dd4"]}, "06": {"paths": [], "paths_more": 0, "commits": ["af84ebceca21", "0a0cb45e49f5"]}}, "base_reviewed": "b647293b00a7566681b3814dfa9cedc260e3e57f", "record_lint": {"records": [".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/architecture.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/boundary-patterns.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-boundary-review-per-release.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/nonfunctional-requirements.md", ".prawduct/changes/boundary-coverage-transfer.md", ".prawduct/changes/boundary-review-per-release.md", ".prawduct/owner-rulings.md", ".prawduct/runbooks/cut-and-publish-a-plugin-release.md", "documentation/release-process.md", "pl